1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,964
Part Three gave us a finite search and a theorem explaining when it
must succeed.

2
00:00:05,486 --> 00:00:09,498
The search still needs justified intervals at its sample addresses.

3
00:00:10,021 --> 00:00:13,670
Now we start building those inputs from the positive integers.

4
00:00:14,192 --> 00:00:20,908
Primes and their powers will supply weights, and a simple ramp will
spread each weight into a contribution to a sum.

5
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:27,042
We will then center that sum to obtain the statistic used by this
repository, G_z.

6
00:00:27,565 --> 00:00:32,038
Every computational component will have an actual ACL2 definition.

7
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:39,627
The quantities those intervals represent are real mathematical
values, so we will keep their interpretation explicit.

8
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:45,617
This is Part Four of Landau Windows ACL Two: prime powers and a
smoothed signal.

9
00:00:46,139 --> 00:00:52,564
The aim is to replace an unexplained numerical box with a finite
construction whose accuracy we can justify.

10
00:00:53,086 --> 00:00:56,820
That is another concrete part of the route toward Peano Arithmetic.

11
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:04,009
A prime is a whole number at least two whose only positive divisors
are one and itself.

12
00:01:04,532 --> 00:01:08,653
A prime power is a prime raised to a positive whole-number exponent.

13
00:01:09,176 --> 00:01:13,418
Two, four, eight, and sixteen are powers of the same prime, two.

14
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:17,577
Thirteen also counts: it is thirteen to the first power.

15
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:22,864
Six does not count, because its prime factors include both two and
three.

16
00:01:23,386 --> 00:01:27,132
One does not count either; the exponent must be positive.

17
00:01:27,655 --> 00:01:33,448
Sixteen gives a useful warning: it is also four squared, but four is
not a prime base.

18
00:01:33,971 --> 00:01:37,608
The prime-power description we want is two to the fourth power.

19
00:01:38,130 --> 00:01:43,524
The weight we will attach is called the von Mangoldt function,
written with a capital Lambda.

20
00:01:44,047 --> 00:01:48,495
At a prime power, its value is the natural logarithm of the prime
base.

21
00:01:49,018 --> 00:01:52,133
At other positive integers, its value is zero.

22
00:01:52,656 --> 00:01:59,524
So every positive power of two receives the weight log two… not the
logarithm of the whole power.

23
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:06,677
We will explain the logarithm calculation shortly; first we need a
bounded way to recognize the prime powers.

24
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:11,285
Start with the power operation itself.

25
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:15,371
Two to the fourth power means multiply four copies of two.

26
00:02:15,894 --> 00:02:22,947
Build it one factor at a time: two… four… eight… sixteen.

27
00:02:23,470 --> 00:02:26,621
The zero exponent supplies the initial value one.

28
00:02:27,144 --> 00:02:31,459
Here is the repository's definition of lw-rational-power.

29
00:02:31,982 --> 00:02:36,527
The base may be rational, but the exponent we are using is a natural
number.

30
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:43,305
This function computes repeated multiplication; fractional-exponent
root bounds are a later construction.

31
00:02:43,827 --> 00:02:48,894
The recursive call reduces the exponent by one, then multiplies by
the base.

32
00:02:49,417 --> 00:02:53,683
The measure declaration identifies the decreasing natural-number
counter.

33
00:02:54,206 --> 00:02:58,182
For our example, the full call returns the integer sixteen.

34
00:02:58,705 --> 00:03:03,662
We can now compare a candidate power with a target number using
exact arithmetic.

35
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:07,752
Next check the proposed base.

36
00:03:08,274 --> 00:03:12,723
A trial divisor divides a number exactly when the remainder is zero.

37
00:03:13,245 --> 00:03:18,421
The mod operation gives that remainder; we already used it in the
clock example.

38
00:03:18,944 --> 00:03:24,398
For example, two divides sixteen with remainder zero, so sixteen is
not prime.

39
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:30,121
The function lw-no-divisor tries divisors from a supplied bound down
to two.

40
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:35,565
Its conjunction stops successfully only when none of those trials
divides the number.

41
00:03:36,088 --> 00:03:39,942
Here is the complete definition, including its decreasing counter.

42
00:03:40,465 --> 00:03:48,223
Now lw-primep first requires an integer at least two, and checks all
smaller candidates from one less than the number.

43
00:03:48,745 --> 00:03:52,127
For two, there are no trials at least two left to check.

44
00:03:52,650 --> 00:03:56,492
For thirteen, none of the trials from twelve down to two divides it.

45
00:03:57,015 --> 00:04:04,700
The project proves that this test rules out every proper divisor, so
it recognizes the ordinary mathematical notion of prime.

46
00:04:05,223 --> 00:04:09,926
This is a simple finite test; its role here is to be explicit and
justified.

47
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:14,176
Suppose the target is sixteen.

48
00:04:14,698 --> 00:04:20,577
For a fixed base, lw-power-exponent tries positive exponents down
from a supplied limit.

49
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:26,106
It compares the computed power with the target and returns the
matching exponent, or nil.

50
00:04:26,629 --> 00:04:33,211
The project proves that any returned exponent really gives the
target, and that a match within the bound will be found.

51
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:37,806
Now read the complete definition of lw-prime-power-search.

52
00:04:38,329 --> 00:04:43,141
It tries candidate bases in descending order, beginning from its
input bound.

53
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:49,223
At each candidate, it first checks primality… then looks for an
exponent.

54
00:04:49,745 --> 00:04:54,945
If a match exists, it returns the pair consisting of the prime base
and the exponent.

55
00:04:55,468 --> 00:04:58,898
Otherwise it decreases the base candidate and continues.

56
00:04:59,421 --> 00:05:01,348
Why can the bounds be finite?

57
00:05:01,871 --> 00:05:08,174
For a prime base and a positive exponent, both the base and the
exponent are at most the resulting prime power.

58
00:05:08,697 --> 00:05:12,830
The project proves those bounds and uses them in its completeness
theorem.

59
00:05:13,353 --> 00:05:19,026
The short wrapper lw-prime-power-data starts both bounded searches
from the target number.

60
00:05:19,548 --> 00:05:27,661
Sixteen returns the pair two and four… thirteen returns thirteen and
one… and six returns nil.

61
00:05:28,183 --> 00:05:31,747
That is enough information to choose the right logarithmic weight.

62
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:37,382
The logarithm in this construction is the natural logarithm.

63
00:05:37,905 --> 00:05:43,311
For an input at least one, it is the area under the curve one over
t, from one to the input.

64
00:05:43,833 --> 00:05:46,391
Use input two as a small example.

65
00:05:46,914 --> 00:05:51,883
Split the interval from one to two into four equal pieces, each of
width one quarter.

66
00:05:52,406 --> 00:05:58,139
The curve decreases, so a rectangle using the right endpoint lies
below the area on its piece.

67
00:05:58,662 --> 00:06:01,789
A rectangle using the left endpoint lies above it.

68
00:06:02,312 --> 00:06:10,150
Add the four smaller rectangles to obtain a lower bound… and the
four larger rectangles to obtain an upper bound.

69
00:06:10,673 --> 00:06:13,655
Those rectangle areas are rational numbers.

70
00:06:14,177 --> 00:06:18,335
The lower sum is five hundred and thirty-three over eight hundred
and forty.

71
00:06:18,858 --> 00:06:22,761
The upper sum is three hundred and nineteen over four hundred and
twenty.

72
00:06:23,283 --> 00:06:25,708
The true log of two lies between them.

73
00:06:26,230 --> 00:06:30,133
Finer partitions give a way to control the gap between the bounds.

74
00:06:30,656 --> 00:06:39,335
The area interpretation is a mathematical theorem; the arithmetic
calculation consists of the finite rational sums we are about to
define.

75
00:06:41,467 --> 00:06:43,927
Here is lw-log-rectangles.

76
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:50,208
Its inputs are the current starting point, the width of one piece,
and the number of pieces remaining.

77
00:06:50,730 --> 00:06:54,221
The empty sum is the exact interval from zero to zero.

78
00:06:54,744 --> 00:07:00,138
For one piece, the lower area is step divided by the quantity start
plus step.

79
00:07:00,661 --> 00:07:03,521
The upper area is step divided by start.

80
00:07:04,044 --> 00:07:08,008
Cons stores those two endpoints in the order we learned in Part Two.

81
00:07:08,530 --> 00:07:13,791
The recursive call moves the starting point one step to the right
and uses one fewer piece.

82
00:07:14,314 --> 00:07:17,938
Interval addition combines this piece with the remaining pieces.

83
00:07:18,461 --> 00:07:21,855
The code therefore implements the rectangle picture directly.

84
00:07:22,377 --> 00:07:29,795
For starting point one, step one quarter, and four pieces, the
checked result is exactly the rational interval we just displayed.

85
00:07:30,318 --> 00:07:34,512
The project also proves formulas and bounds for the interval's full
width.

86
00:07:35,035 --> 00:07:40,223
We still need a rule that chooses a sufficient number of pieces for
a requested precision.

87
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:46,694
Let epsilon be a positive rational bound on the requested interval
width.

88
00:07:47,217 --> 00:07:50,308
We want the piece width to be no larger than epsilon.

89
00:07:50,830 --> 00:07:59,352
The helper lw-precision-fuel uses the numerator of the width and the
denominator of epsilon to produce a safe natural-number work bound.

90
00:07:59,874 --> 00:08:03,474
Numerator and denominator refer to the reduced fractions.

91
00:08:03,997 --> 00:08:06,615
This is a deliberately conservative choice.

92
00:08:07,138 --> 00:08:12,580
The function lw-log-count adds one, ensuring that the rectangle
count is positive.

93
00:08:13,103 --> 00:08:18,965
For input two and epsilon one quarter, the fuel is four… and the
count is five.

94
00:08:19,488 --> 00:08:21,924
Now read lw-log-bounds.

95
00:08:22,447 --> 00:08:27,465
Its let-star bindings first compute the count, then compute the step
using that count.

96
00:08:27,988 --> 00:08:31,176
The step is the input minus one, divided by the count.

97
00:08:31,698 --> 00:08:34,741
The final call sums the rectangles starting at one.

98
00:08:35,264 --> 00:08:40,185
The project proves that the returned interval is valid and has width
at most epsilon.

99
00:08:40,708 --> 00:08:46,720
For epsilon one, the actual output at two is the interval from seven
twelfths to five sixths.

100
00:08:47,242 --> 00:08:51,836
For epsilon one quarter, the automatic count gives the narrower
interval shown.

101
00:08:52,359 --> 00:08:58,323
The four-rectangle picture introduced the idea; this function
supplies the proved automatic choice.

102
00:09:00,467 --> 00:09:05,376
We can now define the von Mangoldt evaluator without leaving its
logarithm unexplained.

103
00:09:05,898 --> 00:09:08,165
Read lw-mangoldt-bounds.

104
00:09:08,688 --> 00:09:10,761
It asks for prime-power data.

105
00:09:11,283 --> 00:09:18,289
If the search returns a pair, car selects the prime base and lw-log-
bounds encloses its natural logarithm.

106
00:09:18,812 --> 00:09:23,103
If the search returns nil, the weight interval is exactly zero.

107
00:09:23,626 --> 00:09:30,777
For sixteen, the base is two, so epsilon one gives the same interval
as log two: seven twelfths to five sixths.

108
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:34,900
For six, the output is the exact pair zero and zero.

109
00:09:35,423 --> 00:09:41,459
The exponent tells us that the input is a prime power, but it does
not multiply the logarithmic weight.

110
00:09:41,982 --> 00:09:46,333
Look at two, four, eight, and sixteen: all receive log two.

111
00:09:46,856 --> 00:09:54,783
The project proves the arithmetic width guarantee and separately
identifies the represented real weight with the standard von
Mangoldt function.

112
00:09:55,306 --> 00:09:59,003
We have a finite, justified source for the weights in our sum.

113
00:10:01,133 --> 00:10:06,018
Now fix a positive integer n and let x move along the horizontal
axis.

114
00:10:06,541 --> 00:10:11,886
The coefficient for that index is the larger of zero and x divided
by n minus one.

115
00:10:12,409 --> 00:10:15,464
Before x reaches n, the coefficient is zero.

116
00:10:15,986 --> 00:10:18,871
At x equal to n, it is still zero.

117
00:10:19,394 --> 00:10:22,048
After that point, it grows linearly.

118
00:10:22,571 --> 00:10:26,280
Here is the complete one-line definition of lw-ramp.

119
00:10:26,803 --> 00:10:34,261
For n equal to four, move x from three and a half… to four… and then
to four and a half.

120
00:10:34,783 --> 00:10:40,677
The corresponding coefficients are zero… zero… and one eighth.

121
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:46,412
The maximum operation is what keeps the contribution turned off
before its starting point.

122
00:10:46,935 --> 00:10:50,292
We multiply this ramp by the von Mangoldt weight at n.

123
00:10:50,815 --> 00:10:55,991
The prime-power pattern supplies the weight, and the ramp supplies
the dependence on x.

124
00:10:58,133 --> 00:11:01,733
Add those weighted ramps over the positive integers up to x.

125
00:11:02,256 --> 00:11:06,159
The repository calls the resulting smoothed sum S_z.

126
00:11:06,682 --> 00:11:11,324
The upper limit is the floor of x, the largest whole number not
exceeding x.

127
00:11:11,847 --> 00:11:14,889
For x equal to nine halves, the floor is four.

128
00:11:15,412 --> 00:11:19,606
The term at one is zero because the von Mangoldt weight at one is
zero.

129
00:11:20,129 --> 00:11:24,553
At two, the ramp coefficient is five quarters and the weight is log
two.

130
00:11:25,076 --> 00:11:29,209
At three, the coefficient is one half and the weight is log three.

131
00:11:29,732 --> 00:11:33,865
At four, the coefficient is one eighth and the weight is log two
again.

132
00:11:34,388 --> 00:11:38,958
Combine the two copies of log two: their coefficients add to eleven
eighths.

133
00:11:39,480 --> 00:11:45,432
So the whole sum at nine halves is eleven eighths times log two,
plus one half times log three.

134
00:11:45,955 --> 00:11:51,724
This is a finite weighted sum, built from the classifications and
coefficients already explained.

135
00:11:52,247 --> 00:11:57,350
We will now arrange the executable calculation so its cutoff is also
explicit.

136
00:11:59,467 --> 00:12:03,600
The actual evaluator uses a convenient rational-arithmetic cutoff.

137
00:12:04,123 --> 00:12:11,141
For a rational x at least one, its positive numerator is at least x,
because the denominator is a positive integer.

138
00:12:11,664 --> 00:12:17,906
Here is lw-signal-count: take that numerator's absolute value and
keep the result natural.

139
00:12:18,429 --> 00:12:21,095
For nine halves, the chosen count is nine.

140
00:12:21,618 --> 00:12:26,006
That is larger than the floor four, so the loop includes extra
indices.

141
00:12:26,529 --> 00:12:30,020
But every index at least x has ramp coefficient zero.

142
00:12:30,542 --> 00:12:34,603
In particular, indices five through nine add nothing to our example.

143
00:12:35,126 --> 00:12:39,780
The teaching book proves the zero-padding fact under its domain
hypotheses.

144
00:12:40,303 --> 00:12:44,691
This lets us use a simple finite counter while preserving the
intended sum.

145
00:12:45,214 --> 00:12:50,026
A cutoff that failed to cover all active terms would need a
different justification.

146
00:12:50,548 --> 00:12:54,209
Here the numerator bound supplies the coverage guarantee we need.

147
00:12:56,333 --> 00:12:58,830
Read lw-smoothed-sum-bounds.

148
00:12:59,353 --> 00:13:04,638
The inputs are x, the chosen count, and the precision requested from
each weight evaluator.

149
00:13:05,161 --> 00:13:08,615
At count zero, the sum is the exact interval zero.

150
00:13:09,138 --> 00:13:15,695
At a positive count, compute the ramp, enclose the corresponding
weight, and scale that interval by the ramp.

151
00:13:16,218 --> 00:13:19,539
Then add the recursive sum over the smaller indices.

152
00:13:20,062 --> 00:13:24,498
All interval operations in this definition were justified in Part
Two.

153
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:32,270
For our padded count nine and primitive epsilon one, the returned
interval is displayed with its exact rational endpoints.

154
00:13:32,792 --> 00:13:41,241
The teaching book checks that it equals the interval obtained from
eleven eighths times the log-two interval, plus one half times the
log-three interval.

155
00:13:41,764 --> 00:13:43,582
How do the errors accumulate?

156
00:13:44,105 --> 00:13:48,129
Every ramp is nonnegative and at most x on the domain we use.

157
00:13:48,652 --> 00:13:54,845
If each weight interval has width at most epsilon, each scaled term
has width at most x times epsilon.

158
00:13:55,368 --> 00:14:00,992
With at most count terms, the full sum has width at most count times
x times epsilon.

159
00:14:01,515 --> 00:14:06,473
This bound is conservative, but it works uniformly and gives us an
explicit budget.

160
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:12,297
The ramp picture also explains why this smoothing is useful.

161
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:16,917
A step contribution would jump when a new prime power entered the
sum.

162
00:14:17,439 --> 00:14:21,597
A ramp contribution begins with value zero and then grows.

163
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:23,744
Move x across four.

164
00:14:24,267 --> 00:14:29,479
The new contribution from four joins at zero, so it does not create
a jump in the sum.

165
00:14:30,002 --> 00:14:33,068
It can create a corner, where the slope changes.

166
00:14:33,591 --> 00:14:39,045
On any fixed bounded range, choose one finite cutoff that covers the
entire range.

167
00:14:39,568 --> 00:14:44,114
The real sum is then a fixed finite sum of continuous ramps with
fixed weights.

168
00:14:44,636 --> 00:14:47,412
That explains its continuity on that range.

169
00:14:47,935 --> 00:14:55,377
The graph is a visualization of the real sum; certified numerical
samples still come from the rational interval evaluator.

170
00:14:55,900 --> 00:15:02,567
The repository supplies explicit interpretation and continuity
proofs for the classical statistic used later.

171
00:15:04,700 --> 00:15:09,839
To center the sum, we need one more constant: Euler's constant,
written gamma.

172
00:15:10,362 --> 00:15:15,502
It is defined by the limiting difference between a harmonic sum and
the natural logarithm.

173
00:15:16,024 --> 00:15:23,164
The harmonic sum at a positive whole number is one, plus one half,
plus one third, and so on up to its reciprocal.

174
00:15:23,686 --> 00:15:28,826
Here is lw-harmonic, the exact rational recursion for that finite
sum.

175
00:15:29,348 --> 00:15:37,808
Build its first values: one… three halves… eleven sixths… and
twenty-five twelfths.

176
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:45,458
Subtracting log of the count gives a sequence approaching gamma,
whose familiar decimal begins zero point five seven seven.

177
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:50,853
We use a finite inequality to turn that limiting definition into
usable bounds.

178
00:15:51,376 --> 00:16:01,020
For a positive count, gamma lies between the harmonic sum minus log
of the count minus its reciprocal… and the harmonic sum minus log of
the count.

179
00:16:01,542 --> 00:16:04,573
The gap in this ideal interval is one over the count.

180
00:16:05,095 --> 00:16:10,586
The logarithm itself has a rational enclosure, so we must propagate
that uncertainty too.

181
00:16:11,109 --> 00:16:17,873
We are enclosing a real constant with rational endpoints, rather
than asserting that the constant is one of those endpoints.

182
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,303
Read lw-euler-from-log.

183
00:16:22,826 --> 00:16:29,165
For the lower endpoint, subtract the upper logarithm bound and the
reciprocal of the count from the harmonic sum.

184
00:16:29,688 --> 00:16:33,385
For the upper endpoint, subtract the lower logarithm bound.

185
00:16:33,908 --> 00:16:37,447
This is the crossed-endpoint subtraction rule from Part Two.

186
00:16:37,970 --> 00:16:42,370
The resulting width is the logarithm interval's width, plus one over
the count.

187
00:16:42,892 --> 00:16:49,002
To make that total at most epsilon, the evaluator budgets at most
half of epsilon for each part.

188
00:16:49,524 --> 00:16:55,318
The helper lw-euler-count chooses one plus the safe work bound for
width two and epsilon.

189
00:16:55,841 --> 00:17:00,520
The project proves that its reciprocal is at most epsilon divided by
two.

190
00:17:01,042 --> 00:17:07,612
Finally, lw-euler-bounds calls the logarithm evaluator at half
epsilon and combines the results.

191
00:17:08,135 --> 00:17:12,402
Every ingredient is now an explicit finite rational computation.

192
00:17:12,924 --> 00:17:16,161
The arithmetic book proves validity and the width bound.

193
00:17:16,683 --> 00:17:24,174
The real-interpretation theorem connects those endpoints to Euler's
constant using the classical harmonic-logarithm inequality.

194
00:17:24,697 --> 00:17:30,515
That distinction between a computation and its meaning is the same
one we kept in the earlier episodes.

195
00:17:32,633 --> 00:17:35,991
We can now read the complete definition of G_z.

196
00:17:36,514 --> 00:17:39,071
Begin with the smoothed sum S_z.

197
00:17:39,594 --> 00:17:42,842
Subtract x times the natural logarithm of x.

198
00:17:43,365 --> 00:17:45,862
Then add x times one plus gamma.

199
00:17:46,385 --> 00:17:50,870
This is the exact normalization used by the repository's formal
statement.

200
00:17:51,392 --> 00:17:58,176
The normalization subtracts x times log x… minus x times the
quantity one plus gamma.

201
00:17:58,698 --> 00:18:02,892
Subtracting that main term leaves the centered gap G_z.

202
00:18:03,415 --> 00:18:07,997
The later Landau argument connects the growth of this gap with zeta
zeros.

203
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:13,065
Here our task is to construct its interval faithfully and keep track
of the error.

204
00:18:13,588 --> 00:18:18,158
We already have the smoothed-sum bounds, logarithm bounds, and gamma
bounds.

205
00:18:18,680 --> 00:18:23,420
Addition, subtraction, and rational scaling put those ingredients
together.

206
00:18:25,533 --> 00:18:28,212
Here is lw-gz-bounds-raw.

207
00:18:28,735 --> 00:18:33,826
The first part subtracts the interval for x times log x from the
smoothed-sum interval.

208
00:18:34,348 --> 00:18:38,155
The second part adds the interval for x times one plus gamma.

209
00:18:38,677 --> 00:18:42,423
The count must cover x, and the primitive tolerance is positive.

210
00:18:42,945 --> 00:18:50,303
The interpretation theorem identifies the resulting interval with
the actual G_z value under those domain conditions.

211
00:18:50,826 --> 00:18:58,595
Let us run a small, deliberately coarse example: x equals two, count
equals two, and primitive epsilon equals one.

212
00:18:59,118 --> 00:19:05,288
At x equal to two, the smoothed prime-power sum is zero, because the
new ramp at two is still zero.

213
00:19:05,811 --> 00:19:10,962
The remaining logarithm and gamma calculations give the exact
rational endpoints shown.

214
00:19:11,485 --> 00:19:16,018
The lower endpoint is positive; that is checked as a theorem in the
teaching book.

215
00:19:16,541 --> 00:19:19,511
This is one certified sample of the statistic.

216
00:19:20,033 --> 00:19:27,900
The window argument will require the corresponding evaluator
guarantees across its whole range, together with its mathematical
hypotheses.

217
00:19:30,033 --> 00:19:33,088
We still need control of the complete interval's width.

218
00:19:33,611 --> 00:19:38,980
The one-line helper lw-interval-width records upper endpoint minus
lower endpoint.

219
00:19:39,503 --> 00:19:43,139
The sum contributes at most count times x times epsilon.

220
00:19:43,662 --> 00:19:52,409
The scaled logarithm contributes at most x times epsilon… and the
gamma correction contributes at most another x times epsilon.

221
00:19:52,932 --> 00:19:57,768
So the total width is at most count plus two, multiplied by x and
epsilon.

222
00:19:58,291 --> 00:20:00,679
The project proves that general bound.

223
00:20:01,202 --> 00:20:06,559
For x equal to nine halves and count nine, the multiplying factor is
ninety-nine halves.

224
00:20:07,082 --> 00:20:10,015
Suppose we want final width at most one quarter.

225
00:20:10,538 --> 00:20:21,103
Divide the target by one plus that factor to choose epsilon one over
two hundred and two… giving width at most ninety-nine over four
hundred and four, below one quarter.

226
00:20:21,626 --> 00:20:26,777
The teaching theorem on screen establishes the desired width from
the general guarantee.

227
00:20:27,300 --> 00:20:34,064
We do not need to display the potentially large endpoint fractions
to prove that this requested precision is sufficient.

228
00:20:34,586 --> 00:20:40,259
The result is a family-level accuracy guarantee, beyond the single
coarse run we just watched.

229
00:20:40,782 --> 00:20:45,630
This is how the error budget in Part Three can be supplied by an
actual evaluator.

230
00:20:47,767 --> 00:20:52,203
We began with positive integers and recognized primes and prime
powers.

231
00:20:52,726 --> 00:20:55,138
The prime base chose a logarithmic weight.

232
00:20:55,661 --> 00:21:01,297
Rational rectangle sums enclosed that weight, and ramps assembled
the finite smoothed sum.

233
00:21:01,820 --> 00:21:07,492
Harmonic and logarithm bounds enclosed gamma, and the centering
formula produced G_z.

234
00:21:08,015 --> 00:21:14,973
The project carries both arithmetic width guarantees and explicit
theorems identifying the represented real quantities.

235
00:21:15,495 --> 00:21:20,635
Those are the ingredients that turn the earlier search into a search
over this particular signal.

236
00:21:21,158 --> 00:21:29,085
We have focused on the statistic itself; fractional-power ceiling
evaluation and further transport bounds have their own
constructions.

237
00:21:29,608 --> 00:21:37,159
Next we return to the Landau question: why can a one-sided bound on
recurring windows exclude zeros in a forbidden region?

238
00:21:37,682 --> 00:21:44,288
The evaluator now has a defined job in that argument, rather than
appearing as a mysterious numerical oracle.

239
00:21:44,811 --> 00:21:50,932
The full reduction keeps the analytic hypotheses and the arithmetic
interpretation attached to that job.
